QUICKSTART
----------

NOTE: KLone needs GNU make in order to be built, which is not the default 'make'
      on most *BSD systems (usually you will find it as 'gmake' or 'gnumake').

1) Configure and build KLone:

    $ ./configure && ${GNUMAKE}

2) Import the example web-application

    [ on Windows edit doc/example-site/etc/kloned.conf and remove syslog 
      logging paragraph ]

    $ ${GNUMAKE} depend
    $ cd site && ../src/tools/klone/klone -c import ../doc/example-site
    $ cd .. && ${GNUMAKE}
    $ su
    # ${GNUMAKE} install

3) Launch the KLone executable:

    [ on Windows register the service with "kloned -i" and start it using
      the Services console ]

    $ kloned

4) Start your web browser:

    [ start your web browser and go to http://localhost:8080/ ]


For further info and documentation, see http://www.koanlogic.com/klone

MODERN LINUX / WSL BUILD
-----------------------

Supported baseline: Linux, Python 3, GNU make/GCC, OpenSSL 3 and zlib. On Ubuntu:

    sudo apt-get update
    sudo apt-get install build-essential clang libclang-rt-dev libssl-dev zlib1g-dev python3 git patch
    python3 build/build.py --test
    python3 build/build.py --sanitize --test

On Windows, open PowerShell in this repository and run the Python commands
through WSL, for example:

    wsl -d Ubuntu -- python3 build/build.py --test

The runner downloads MaKL and LibU at the exact commits in
build/dependencies.json. Existing dependency checkouts must match those commits
and have no tracked modifications (CRLF-only changes are normalized). It copies
source files into .build/release/source or .build/sanitize/source and applies the
reviewable build/makl-modern.patch there. Your checkout is not reconfigured or
compiled in place. --offline uses already downloaded dependencies; --incremental
reuses generated files during development. Omit --incremental for clean checks.

The resulting daemon is .build/<mode>/source/src/kloned/kloned. Logs and
build-info.json are in .build/<mode>. The metadata records dependency commits,
a normalized source fingerprint, compiler and OpenSSL versions, and C flags.
These reproduce the source inputs; identical binaries across different compiler
or distribution versions are not guaranteed. No system service is installed.

--test imports a small test-only dynamic page into the generated build, starts
the daemon on temporary localhost ports, and checks static/HEAD responses,
malformed headers and URIs, TLS negotiation, cookie flags, and memory/client-side
session persistence. The test page is not added to the normal build. Test
certificates and listeners are temporary. Never publish the test-enabled binary.

The build currently disables C++ pages. Native Windows and the old CyaSSL backend
are not covered by the full build; Windows uses the tested WSL path. Some warnings
remain in legacy code and pinned dependencies. CI runs the Linux build and tests
in normal and sanitizer configurations.

REGRESSION AND FUZZ TESTS
------------------------

With Python 3 and Clang, the dependency-free utility suite runs on Windows/Linux:

    python test/run_regressions.py --sanitize

It compiles selected production functions verbatim as C99 with warnings treated
as errors. LibU and network collaborators are test doubles; production MD5 is
linked directly. This complements the full-server tests, not replaces them.

After a full build, run a bounded libFuzzer test of actual parser/utility code:

    python3 test/run_fuzz.py --source .build/release/source --runs 10000

Fuzz logs, corpus and any crash inputs are kept inside the selected build folder.
Longer fuzz campaigns and platform coverage remain useful follow-up work.

SECURITY AND COMPATIBILITY CHANGES
----------------------------------

* OpenSSL contexts use owned opaque objects and public accessors. TLS negotiates
  TLS 1.2 or newer; ephemeral 512-bit RSA and the default 1024-bit DH group are no
  longer used. OpenSSL selects suitable DH parameters automatically unless an
  explicit dh_file is configured.
* Session IDs contain 128 bits of cryptographic randomness. Client-session MACs
  default to HMAC-SHA256, use checked OpenSSL EVP_MAC calls, and compare tags in
  constant time. Client-session IV and encoded-data bounds are checked.
* Session cookies use Path=/, HttpOnly and SameSite=Lax, plus Secure on direct
  TLS connections. The old response_set_cookie API preserves application-cookie
  behavior; response_set_cookie_ex exposes explicit security flags. Deletion
  cookies retain their scope and security attributes. Third-party login/embedding
  flows may need an explicit SameSite policy; proxy TLS is not inferred from
  untrusted forwarded headers.
* HTTP input rejects duplicate Content-Length, unsupported Transfer-Encoding,
  invalid field names/control characters, folded headers, unterminated headers,
  numeric overflow, and decoded NULs in URI paths. Limits: 256 fields, 8192 bytes
  per header line, 65536 aggregate bytes. URL path '+' and escaped '?' are preserved.
  Chunked request bodies remain unsupported and are rejected explicitly.
* Capacity-aware u_{url,hex,html,sql}ncpy_s APIs reject insufficient destinations.
  Internal URL/hex callers use them; legacy public APIs remain available for
  compatibility and still require correctly sized caller buffers. SQL quote
  encoding now processes the complete input instead of truncating after quotes.

Existing client-side sessions may need renewal when the default MAC changes.
The client-session format still uses encrypt-then-MAC around AES-CBC; migrating
that versioned wire format to AEAD, replacing MaKL, and extending native-platform
coverage are larger follow-up changes, rather than claims of this update.
